    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 081803\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n336 AM PDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n*  Above-normal temperatures expected mid-to-late week.\n\n*  Breezy south-southwesterly wind gusts expected across the region \n   Friday through the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Monday.\n\nAn elongated area of high pressure will build over the region \nthrough the week, which will result in warming temperatures \ntemperatures. By Wednesday, temperatures will climb to above-\nnormal levels and then remain above-normal through at least \nSaturday. The heat will peak on Thursday as HeatRisk reaches\nMajor in desert valleys with the aforementioned high centered \noverhead. As a result, an Extreme Heat Warning has been issued for\nThursday for Death Valley, the Las Vegas Valley, Pahrump Valley, \nMoapa Valley, Colorado River Valley, southern Mohave County, and \nsoutheastern San Bernardino County. Late-week into the weekend, \ntemperatures will gradually cool, with Minor-to-Moderate HeatRisk \nin many locations. Despite the high over the Southwest US, the \nflow will not be favorable for monsoonal moisture to return to the\nregion and generally a dry forecast is expected. However, weak \nmoisture squished under the upper-level ridge could produce \nisolated thunderstorms in the Sierra and terrain of the southern \nGreat Basin Wednesday and Thursday due to strong daytime heating \nand weak upslope flow. Confidence in convective development and\nplacement is low at this time.\n\nDecent model agreement that an upper-level trough will push through \nthe Pacific Northwest toward the end of the week. While the system \nwill remain too far north to result in precipitation over the \nregion, an increasing pressure gradient will bring breezy south-to\nsouthwest-winds to the region. Widespread gusts 25-35 MPH are \nexpected, with over a 50% chance of gusts over 40 MPH in the \nwestern Mojave Desert Friday and Saturday. There is also a signal \nfor enhanced southerly winds up the Colorado River Valley, peaking\non Saturday, which could impact boaters with winds and waves. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Light \nwinds generally following typical diurnal directional trends will \ncontinue through the TAF period. Speeds will generally remain below \n8 knots, although a few gusts of 12 to 15 knots are possible during \nthe period of maximum heating this afternoon. VFR conditions will \nprevail through Tuesday with only a FEW to SCT mid- and high-level \nclouds at times. Temperatures should top out right around 100F by \nmid to late afternoon. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Favorable aviation \nconditions will persist across the region through Tuesday with winds \ngenerally following typical daily directional patterns and speeds \nremaining 12 knots or less. VFR conditions will prevail with only a \nFEW to SCT mid- and high-level clouds streaming across the region at \ntimes.  \n\n&&\n\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Nickerson/Soulat\nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
